n composition step and a different failure story from a single export. I would ship single-suite schedules first and treat the digest as its own piece of work.\\n\\nI've written this up as a plan with four nodes — the delivery log is a blocking dependency for the digest node, so it cannot be quietly skipped.\"},{\"kind\":\"usage\",\"input\":11400,\"output\":236,\"cache_read\":9700,\"cache_write\":180}]},{\"id\":\"review-pull-request\",\"triggers\":[\"review\",\"pull request\",\"pr \",\"diff\",\"look at the changes\"],\"steps\":[{\"kind\":\"thinking\",\"text\":\"The PR reworks eval token budgets per model family, and three surfaces render remaining tokens. If one eval's retry can shift the calculation, every surface lies at once. Let me read the diff before saying anything.\"},{\"kind\":\"say\",\"text\":\"Reading the diff on **#412 — Cap eval-run token budget per model family**.\\n\\n\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"args\":{\"path\":\"evalkit/budget.py\"},\"result\":\"class EvalBudget:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"All spend math goes through the ledger so the run card, the\\n    grader and the tests share one definition of remaining tokens.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n    def __init__(self, ledger):\\n        self._ledger = ledger\\n\\n    def remaining(self, family, run_group_id):\\n        return self._cap(family) - self._ledger.spent(family)\\n\\n    def _cap(self, family):\\n        return {\\n            \\\"sonnet\\\": 200_000,\\n            \\\"haiku\\\": 80_000,\\n            \\\"opus\\\": 400_000,\\n        }[family]\"},{\"kind\":\"say\",\"text\":\"The arithmetic itself is clean — one cap table, one ledger, remaining tokens derived instead of a shared org pool. What I want to check is whether two evals can race it: `remaining` resolves from the family alone, with no run-group id in the key.\\n\\n\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"args\":{\"pattern\":\"remaining\\\\(\",\"path\":\".\"},\"result\":\"evalkit/budget.py:9:    def remaining(self, family, run_group_id):